WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:01.587
[Fleabag laughing]

00:00:01.587 --> 00:00:02.592
I hate myself.

00:00:02.592 --> 00:00:03.467
We're gonna have sex.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:04.500
Knew it.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.050
Narrator: Usually, the main character

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.410
of the show "Fleabag" is able to speak to

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.490
or look directly at the camera

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.099
without other characters noticing.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:15.933
That is, until this moment.

00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:17.540
Fleabag: Won't last a week.

00:00:18.787 --> 00:00:20.010
Priest: What was that?

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.000
Narrator: Suddenly, this reliable aspect

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.439
of the show is shattered.

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:24.272
[screams]

00:00:24.272 --> 00:00:26.763
But it's not the only tenet that changes.

00:00:26.763 --> 00:00:29.314
In fact, nearly all the rules of the show

00:00:29.314 --> 00:00:31.770
fall apart in this 10-minute sequence,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.972
revealing that these devices
are even more powerful

00:00:34.972 --> 00:00:36.337
when they're taken away.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.660
Understanding the way
that these rules break

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.110
proves why there can never be

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.601
a third season of "Fleabag."

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:44.840
The 10-minute sequence,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.640
which occurs at the exact
center of season two,

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.581
starts at the end of episode three

00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:50.881
with this conversation

00:00:50.881 --> 00:00:52.991
and continues into episode four.

00:00:52.991 --> 00:00:54.990
Fleabag and the Priest go shopping,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.214
go for a walk,

00:00:56.214 --> 00:00:58.038
go to a silent Quaker meeting,

00:00:58.038 --> 00:00:59.191
walk some more,

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:02.150
and then have a conversation
in Fleabag's café.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.535
There are three main devices

00:01:03.535 --> 00:01:05.005
that make this sequence different

00:01:05.005 --> 00:01:08.160
from the eight and a half
episodes that lead up to it.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.700
Let's start with the most obvious:

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.432
breaking the fourth wall.

00:01:11.432 --> 00:01:12.774
The way Fleabag interacts

00:01:12.774 --> 00:01:14.415
with the camera and the audience

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:16.740
is something that immediately
sets the show apart

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.622
from everything else on TV.

00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:20.680
Almost every conversation in the show

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.016
is interrupted by this device.

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:24.962
We get so used to it that Fleabag

00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:26.595
only has to shoot us a look

00:01:26.595 --> 00:01:28.370
and we know what she's thinking.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.505
But as the show goes on,

00:01:29.505 --> 00:01:31.640
we get a better sense
of why she's so obsessed

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.460
with talking to and looking at us.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.400
It's through her closeness with us

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.950
that Fleabag remains
distanced from other people.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.240
Fleabag: I have friends.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.173
Therapist: Oh, so you do
have someone to talk to?

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.503
Fleabag: Yeah.

00:01:46.503 --> 00:01:48.770
Narrator: Through the attention
that she gives the camera,

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.787
she remains partially absent and protected

00:01:51.787 --> 00:01:54.033
from her conversations
with other characters.

00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:55.673
Claire: You're being so quiet.

00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:58.382
Why aren't you saying anything?

00:01:58.382 --> 00:01:59.490
Narrator: But in this moment,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.157
the Priest cuts through this defense.

00:02:02.157 --> 00:02:04.580
Priest: Where did you just go?

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.320
Narrator: Throughout
the 10-minute sequence,

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.460
as Fleabag spends the day with the Priest,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.030
she keeps us in the loop.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.468
But it gets harder for her to keep

00:02:11.468 --> 00:02:13.318
this audience relationship secret

00:02:13.318 --> 00:02:15.235
the longer she's with him.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.474
Take a look at this exchange.

00:02:17.474 --> 00:02:18.690
Fleabag: His neck.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.994
Narrator: Fleabag says that to us.

00:02:20.994 --> 00:02:23.532
Priest: And you never
felt them go somewhere?

00:02:23.532 --> 00:02:24.790
Fleabag: No, they were already gone.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.023
Narrator: Then she accidentally
answers his question to us.

00:02:28.023 --> 00:02:29.385
Fleabag: His beautiful neck.

00:02:29.385 --> 00:02:30.218
Priest: What?

00:02:30.218 --> 00:02:31.493
Narrator: And says what's meant for us

00:02:31.493 --> 00:02:33.341
directly to the Priest.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:35.443
Fleabag: What?

00:02:35.443 --> 00:02:36.980
Priest: You just said,
"His beautiful neck."

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.170
Narrator: This sequence,
which traces the progression

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.022
of a relationship from distant, to close,

00:02:42.022 --> 00:02:44.005
to uncomfortably close,

00:02:44.005 --> 00:02:46.673
challenges her to shed this defense.

00:02:46.673 --> 00:02:48.816
Priest: What is that?

00:02:48.816 --> 00:02:49.777
Fleabag: What? [laughs]

00:02:49.777 --> 00:02:51.443
Priest: That thing that you're doing.

00:02:51.443 --> 00:02:52.587
It's like you disappear.

00:02:52.587 --> 00:02:53.420
Fleabag: What?

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.428
Priest: What are you not telling me?

00:02:54.428 --> 00:02:55.261
Fleabag: Nothing!

00:02:55.261 --> 00:02:56.690
Narrator: Talking to us prevents her

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.450
from getting too close to people,

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.013
and this sequence strips that from her.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:02.037
But her asides aren't

00:03:02.037 --> 00:03:03.870
the only element that breaks down.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.311
The editing style the show is known for

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:09.501
almost entirely disappears
during this sequence.

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:10.670
The easiest way to get a sense

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.010
of how the show is usually shot and edited

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.220
is to look at the dinner scene

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.924
at the top of season two.

00:03:15.924 --> 00:03:17.191
As the scene starts,

00:03:17.191 --> 00:03:20.474
there are 16 cuts in just 30 seconds

00:03:20.474 --> 00:03:22.750
and 11 different types of shots.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.010
Compare that to how this sequence begins.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.184
Just three different shot types

00:03:27.184 --> 00:03:29.770
and only six cuts in 30 seconds.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.160
Now, you might think this difference

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.137
is just because we're comparing
a scene with six people

00:03:34.137 --> 00:03:35.930
to a scene with just two.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.317
But even if you dissect other

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:39.390
two-person conversations
throughout the show,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.996
they look a lot more
like the dinner scene.

00:03:41.996 --> 00:03:43.154
Fleabag: I need to speak to Martin.

00:03:43.154 --> 00:03:44.679
Claire: Martin?
Fleabag: Martin.

00:03:44.679 --> 00:03:45.525
Martin.

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:46.633
Claire: Why on earth would
you want to speak to -

00:03:46.633 --> 00:03:47.466
Fleabag: Martin?

00:03:47.466 --> 00:03:48.930
Narrator: It's not just the speed of cuts

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.701
that changes during the sequence.

00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:52.482
It's also the style.

00:03:52.482 --> 00:03:53.880
Throughout the rest of the show,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.356
editor Gary Dollner uses jump cuts,

00:03:56.356 --> 00:03:58.914
hard audio cuts, and scene transitions

00:03:58.914 --> 00:04:00.261
that cut off characters mid-wor-

00:04:00.261 --> 00:04:01.861
Godmother: Do you know,
the most fascinating thing

00:04:01.861 --> 00:04:03.219
about Father here

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:05.328
is that his mother was originally a lesbi-

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:08.570
Narrator: He also frequently cuts

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:10.843
across the 180-degree line,

00:04:10.843 --> 00:04:12.812
breaking a filmmaking
rule that helps maintain

00:04:12.812 --> 00:04:14.944
a clear sense of where
characters are positioned

00:04:14.944 --> 00:04:16.620
in relation to each other.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.620
In contrast, Fleabag
and the Priest's scenes

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.610
during this sequence are framed and edited

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.114
in a simpler, more fluid way.

00:04:23.114 --> 00:04:24.520
During the first two conversations

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.060
in the house and on the bench,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.506
we get that setup with three
traditional shot types:

00:04:28.506 --> 00:04:30.320
one medium shot on each character

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.580
and one wide shot, which they share.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.850
Unlike the dinner scene,
the camera remains

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.015
on the same side of the characters,

00:04:37.015 --> 00:04:39.450
following the 180-degree rule.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.913
This sequence also opens
with a smooth audio lead-in.

00:04:42.913 --> 00:04:44.046
Fleabag: So, I read your book.

00:04:44.046 --> 00:04:45.129
Priest: OK, great.

00:04:45.129 --> 00:04:46.603
Fleabag: Well, it's got some great twists.

00:04:46.603 --> 00:04:47.700
Narrator: When Fleabag and the Priest

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:48.940
walk down the street,

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.470
they spend a long time sharing

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.355
just this one camera angle from the front.

00:04:53.355 --> 00:04:55.690
Traditionally, editors try to hide cuts

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:57.520
and make scenes feel seamless.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.755
But in "Fleabag," cuts are
jarring and noticeable.

00:05:00.755 --> 00:05:03.290
Fleabag is divided from
the people around her,

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:06.230
so the show's editing
feels fragmented too.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.180
But that all changes during this sequence.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.050
There's one final clue that this sequence

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.481
is operating differently
than the rest of the show.

00:05:13.481 --> 00:05:16.370
[choral theme music]

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.260
This is how the show flashes its title

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.548
throughout the second season.

00:05:19.548 --> 00:05:21.076
Fleabag: This is a love story.

00:05:21.076 --> 00:05:23.925
[choral theme music]

00:05:23.925 --> 00:05:24.892
Amen.

00:05:24.892 --> 00:05:26.927
[choral theme music]

00:05:26.927 --> 00:05:31.413
[choral theme music]
[Fleabag gasps]

00:05:31.413 --> 00:05:33.660
[choral theme music]

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.544
Narrator: This Greek choral singing

00:05:35.544 --> 00:05:38.780
frequently overlays Fleabag's
thoughts about the Priest.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.020
It's a reverent, Catholic-sounding theme,

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.890
but it represents Fleabag's desire

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.940
to violate a core Catholic principle.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.563
Using this sense of irony,

00:05:47.563 --> 00:05:49.432
Fleabag turns her interest in him

00:05:49.432 --> 00:05:51.782
into just another inside joke with us,

00:05:51.782 --> 00:05:53.832
the audience.

00:05:53.832 --> 00:05:55.920
Fleabag: Oh, God, I fancy a priest.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.960
Narrator: But look at the
opening of episode four

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.380
during the game-changing
10-minute sequence.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:01.321
Fleabag: Last one, definitely.

00:06:01.321 --> 00:06:02.746
Priest: Yeah, great.

00:06:02.746 --> 00:06:05.419
[romantic guitar music]

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.679
Narrator: We hear music
that is genuinely romantic.

00:06:11.679 --> 00:06:13.670
When she begins to care more about him

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:15.695
than she does about entertaining us,

00:06:15.695 --> 00:06:17.411
everything shifts.

00:06:17.411 --> 00:06:19.490
So, this sequence stands out,

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.670
but what's the point in
changing the way the show works

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.612
for just 10 minutes?

00:06:23.612 --> 00:06:25.120
As Fleabag heals from grief,

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.441
her dysfunctional
relationship with the audience

00:06:27.441 --> 00:06:28.680
and the filmmaking language

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.920
that constitutes that
relationship transform.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.540
That's why the show ends with Fleabag

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.123
refusing to let us follow behind her.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.800
This choice wouldn't be as striking

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.742
without her 10-minute day
and night with the Priest.

00:06:42.742 --> 00:06:44.926
During "Fleabag"'s perfect sequence,

00:06:44.926 --> 00:06:47.151
we get a sense not just
of why her relationship

00:06:47.151 --> 00:06:48.714
with us has to end,

00:06:48.714 --> 00:06:51.947
but what it's been
preventing her from seeing.

